# monitor-order - the desktop's physical layout, read by script/monitors.sh
#
# One output per line, in the order they sit LEFT TO RIGHT:
#
#     # output   resolution   rate   flags
#     HDMI-1     2560x1440    144    primary
#     eDP-1      1920x1080    60
#
# Only the output name is required. `xrandr --query | grep " connected"` lists
# the names, and `script/monitors.sh --modes` lists what each one can do.
#
# Position, primary and mode are three separate things:
#
#   position   the order of these lines. This alone decides the workspace
#              split - 1-5 on the left screen, 6-10 on the right - so the
#              number keys walk across the desk in the order the screens sit.
#   primary    the `primary` flag. One output. It does NOT move a screen and
#              does NOT change which workspaces it gets; it is what apps mean
#              when they ask for "the main screen". Defaults to the leftmost.
#   mode       resolution and refresh rate. Omit to use whatever the screen
#              says it prefers.
#
# Resolution may carry the rate (2560x1440@144), and `-` or `auto` keeps the
# preferred resolution while still setting a rate or a flag:
#
#     eDP-1      -            48
#     HDMI-1     auto         -      primary
#
# Outputs listed here but not plugged in are skipped, and anything connected
# but not listed is appended on the right - so one file covers docked and
# undocked. With every line commented out, as it ships, everything is
# auto-detected: internal panel first, then whatever xrandr reports.
#
# This is the durable place to put a layout. monitors.sh runs with no
# arguments on every i3 start and every `i3-msg reload`, so a one-off
# `monitors.sh --rate HDMI-1=120` lasts only until the next reload - whereas a
# line in here is re-applied every time.
#
# Uncomment and edit:
#
HDMI-1     1920x1080    120
eDP-1      1920x1080    60     primary
